Before each independent review, fresh-fetch the implementation, review, and ledger histories and reconstruct the full correctness path from current source. Independent review records remain exact-SHA scoped.

## 2.2 One correction domain at a time

A commit should represent one attributable correctness correction or one same-domain review fix. Do not mix unrelated bug families merely because they are nearby in a file.

Same-domain defects discovered while implementing a correction may be fixed in the same commit if:

- they are necessary to make the correction coherent;
- they share the same correctness invariant;
- attribution remains understandable;
- tests distinguish the behaviors.

If discovered after the first commit, add a new child commit. Never amend.

## 2.3 Additive Git history only

Forbidden during the remediation:

- amend;
- rebase of referenced work;
- squash of referenced work;
- force-push;
- rewriting historical remediation SHAs.

Expected shape:

```text
base
  ├─ fix: defect A implementation
  ├─ fix: defect A review follow-up
  ├─ fix: defect B implementation
  ├─ fix: defect A later independent-review follow-up
  └─ ...
```

A later fix for an older defect may appear after newer defect commits. Its commit body/report must identify the reviewed checkpoint and defect ID.

## 2.4 External review precedence

If a new independent review for an already-pushed exact SHA becomes available while later work is in progress:

1. finish the current working tree into a coherent, non-broken state;
2. fresh-fetch the new review record and current remote code;
3. prioritize review blockers by severity;
4. address the old defect with an additive review-fix commit on current HEAD;
5. rerun its relevant regression set;
6. return it to `Independent review: PENDING`.

Priority rule:

1. fresh independent-review P0 blocker;
2. fresh independent-review P1 blocker;
3. current in-progress correction if already at a coherent completion boundary;
4. fresh independent-review P2 blocker;
5. next planned defect.

A destructive/data-loss P0 may interrupt earlier if continuing the current work would increase risk.

## 2.5 Implementation model may self-review, but may not self-close

After each pushed candidate commit, the implementation model should perform a second pass from the modified source and actively search for bypasses. The final report must use language such as:

`SELF-REVIEWED / READY FOR INDEPENDENT REVIEW`

and must not claim:

`CLEAN`

unless reporting a previously established independent closure from the ledger.

## 2.6 Closure record rule

After an exact implementation SHA is independently CLEAN, create/record a closure containing only decisions already established by review/evidence.

A closure record should include:

- defect ID;
- final verdict;
- implementation start SHA;
- final semantic implementation SHA;
- review-fix SHAs;
- exact reviewed checkpoint;
- blocker history and disposition;
- executed tests and results;
- unexecuted tests;
- emulator evidence;
- physical-device evidence;
- residual non-blockers;
- next dependency pointer.

A closure commit must **not** introduce a new blocker-impacting classification, attribution decision, waiver, or semantic finding decision.

---

# 3. Evidence vocabulary and execution discipline

Use only accurate evidence labels:

- `PASS`
- `FAIL`
- `ATTEMPTED NOT COMPLETED`
- `FAIL BEFORE EXECUTION`
- `ADDED NOT EXECUTED`
- `NOT EXECUTED`
- `SOURCE-LEVEL ONLY`
- `SOURCE-LEVEL FIXED`

Never call an unexecuted test PASS.

Physical Samsung SM-A546E evidence must be written exactly:

`NOT EXECUTED — DEVICE UNAVAILABLE`

Luna/Sol/local-model reported PASS is not automatically an independent ChatGPT PASS. Independent review should distinguish source conclusions from execution evidence reproduced in its own environment.

## 3.1 Gradle/build policy

Known project/toolchain characteristics from the prior plan:

- one `app` module;
- Gradle 8.13;
- AGP 8.13.2;
- Kotlin 2.3.0;
- KSP 2.3.4;
- JDK 21;
- `DownloadWorker.kt` is a known Kotlin coroutine compiler hotspot.

During correction loops:

- prefer focused tests;
- use `git diff --check` on every candidate;
- avoid duplicate parallel Gradle processes;
- run serial Gradle execution where shared state can interfere;
- distinguish a compiler/test harness timeout from a semantic test failure;
- do not repeatedly trigger the most expensive full compile after every tiny edit.

For a substantial candidate, normally run as applicable:

```text
git diff --check
:app:kspDebugKotlin
:app:compileDebugKotlin -x lint
focused JVM tests
:app:compileDebugAndroidTestKotlin
focused instrumentation / WorkManager / Room tests
```

Final heavy verification is defined later in this document.

---

# 4. Correctness review method

Every independent review must inspect the current source as a system, not only the changed lines.

For data-integrity/destructive findings reconstruct:

```text
producer
→ identity / authority creation
→ persistence / carrier
→ retry / recovery / process-death path
→ semantic consumer
→ destructive or irreversible side effect
```

For output correctness specifically reconstruct:

```text
yt-dlp producer
→ effective command/arguments
→ output plan
→ operation/execution ownership
→ staging/direct write
→ output provenance
→ move/copy
→ ffmpeg/hard-sub derivative
→ finalPaths
→ quality
→ media publication
→ History insertion/replacement
→ old-media deletion
```

For every finding inspect:

- production wiring;
- all callers;
- stale writers;
- retries;
- cancellation/pause races;
- worker/process death;
- transaction boundaries;
- filesystem effects;
- SAF/provider effects;
- destructive cleanup;
- tests at the real production boundary.

A helper-level proof is insufficient when the bug exists at Worker/Room/WorkManager/Activity lifecycle wiring.

---

# 5. Planning-vs-source precedence

Each detailed defect dossier below contains one or more of:

- `CONFIRMED CURRENT`: independently revalidated against the latest known current source during the recent review cycle;
- `HISTORICALLY CONFIRMED`: established by the original repository review/master plan but not fully re-audited during generation of this document;
- `ROOT-CAUSE HYPOTHESIS`: likely fix boundary that must be checked against fresh source before implementation;
- `PRODUCT DECISION REQUIRED`: code must not silently choose semantics.

The implementation agent must not force the planned correction if fresh current source shows the defect has already changed or the planned mechanism is no longer applicable.

---

# 6. Execution queue — current priority order

This queue intentionally differs from the original F1→F22 ordering because current remediation discovered urgent output/data-integrity work and additional post-review priorities.

The queue is **dependency-aware and asynchronous**. An independent-review gate blocks defects that depend on that gate; it does not freeze all unrelated work. While one candidate awaits independent review, the implementation model may continue with the highest-priority defect whose dependencies are already satisfied. A later independent P0/P1 review blocker preempts new work at the next coherent commit boundary according to Section 11.

## Tier 0 — finish current correctness boundary

1. `BUG-OUTPUT-01` — output provenance and physical output confinement.
2. Independent review/closure of `BUG-OUTPUT-01`.

While step 2 is pending, do **not** start Finding B, but independently actionable defects such as the Player timeline/index crash or historical defects whose dependencies are already satisfied may be implemented as separate attributable candidates.

## Tier 1 — source-less History authorization and F1 close-out

3. `FINDING-B / BUG-BACKUP-01-B` — source-less command History redownload identity, actionable only after BUG-OUTPUT-01 independent CLEAN.
4. Independent review of Finding B.
5. As soon as Finding B is independently CLEAN, perform the **full current-source F1 / BUG-BACKUP-01 close-out review and ledger closure** before any downstream backup/restore defect is allowed to rely on BUG-BACKUP-01 as closed.

While steps 3–5 are pending on independent review/closure, the runner **must continue** unrelated actionable defects under Section 11.2.1. Do not rewrite or rebase that asynchronous implementation history; simply honor the dependency before starting F1-dependent work.

## Tier 2 — high-priority crash, input, identity, and queue correctness

6. Player timeline/index crash — current P1 crash path; keep separate from historical `BUG-PLAYER-01` unless fresh source proves one root cause/state machine.
7. Crash evidence preservation.
8. MainActivity startup/lifecycle crash root cause — only after causal evidence is preserved well enough to diagnose it.
9. Link input/share handling.
10. Canonical media/source identity.
11. History title-only duplicate deletion.
12. `BUG-QUEUE-03` — queue enqueue/durability boundary discovered after the original F1–F22 inventory.
13. Preset LayoutParams crash.
14. Quality degradation semantics — only after an explicit product-contract decision if current behavior is ambiguous.
15. Worker/foreground anomaly — only after synchronized evidence establishes a concrete defect.

## Tier 3 — remaining original baseline defects

This is the default placement for the remaining historical inventory, **not permission to postpone a freshly confirmed destructive P0/P1 and not a global gate behind Tier 0/Tier 1**. During any asynchronous review/dependency wait, when higher-priority items are blocked, and again before starting a lower-severity UI correction, revalidate the historical P0/P1 entries against current source. A freshly confirmed current P0/P1 moves ahead of lower-severity work. Otherwise continue the remaining dependency-eligible original defects while preserving their hard dependencies:

- `BUG-OBSERVE-01`
- `BUG-BACKUP-04`
- `BUG-BACKUP-02`
- `BUG-BACKUP-06`
- `BUG-BACKUP-08`
- `BUG-BACKUP-05`
- `BUG-BACKUP-07`
- `BUG-CLEANUP-01`
- `BUG-BACKUP-03`
- `BUG-KEYWORD-01`
- `BUG-METADATA-02`
- `BUG-METADATA-01`
- `BUG-DATE-01`
- `BUG-DATE-02`
- `BUG-HISTORY-01`
- `BUG-KEYWORD-02`
- `BUG-DUPLICATE-01`
- `BUG-LOCALADD-01`
- original `BUG-PLAYER-01` ordered playback-position persistence if still open
- original `BUG-QUEUE-01` if still applicable after `BUG-QUEUE-03`

The implementation agent must reconcile duplicate/overlapping defect IDs before editing, rather than fixing the same root cause twice under different names.

## Tier 4 — final correctness closure

- full remaining baseline/F2 review after F1 has already been independently closed;
- all open in-scope P0/P1/P2 blockers closed or explicitly policy-waived where allowed;
- final heavy verification;
- Known-Good Baseline procedure.

## Tier 5 — post-correctness feature work

